{:edb/db-before-t 1629 :edb/db-after-t 1630 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534942 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:45:52.401Z" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186099795 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099796 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186099861 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099863 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100353 1078 #edb/ref 17592186100358 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445183 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445189 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445184 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445186 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445182 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445188 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445185 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445187 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445190 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101089 1050 960.0 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101439 1071 "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=-3,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=1.$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101439 1071 "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$f(1) = 2, f^{′}(1) =-3, g(1) = 4, g^{′}(1) = 2$\n\nCompute $\\frac{d}{dx}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x = 1$." 13194139534942 17592186045417 false] [17592186101439 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=1} & =f(1){g}^{′}(1)+g(1){f}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101450 1071 "For the functions $h(x)$ and $z(x)$, you're given the following data:\n$h(5) = 5, h^{′}(5) =-3, z(5) =-6, z^{′}(5) = 8$\n\nIf $w(x) = (h(x) + z(x))(3h(x) - 10)$, compute $w^{′}(5)$." 13194139534942 17592186045417 false] [17592186101450 1071 "For the functions $h(x)$ and $z(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nh(5)=5,\\,{h}^{′}(5)=-3,\\,z(5)=-6,\\,{z}^{′}(5)=8\n$$\n\nIf $w(x)=(h(x)+z(x))(3h(x)-10)$, compute ${w}^{′}(5).$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101450 1074 "The product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=h(x)+z(x)$ and $v=3h(x)-10.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={h}^{′}(x)+{z}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({h}^{′}(x)+{z}^{′}(x))(3h(x)-10)+(h(x)+z(x))(3{h}^{′}(x)) \\\\  & =({h}^{′}(x)+{z}^{′}(x))(3h(x)-10)+3{h}^{′}(x)(h(x)+z(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(5) & =({h}^{′}(5)+{z}^{′}(5))(3h(5)-10)+3{h}^{′}(5)(h(5)+z(5)) \\\\  & =(-3+8)\\cdot (3\\cdot 5-10)+3\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (5+(-6)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101532 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x = 2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $f^{′}(x)$ | $g^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nIf $h(x) = (f(x) + 1)/(2g(x))$, compute $h^{′}(2)$." 13194139534942 17592186045417 false] [17592186101532 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nIf $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)+1}{2g(x)},$ compute ${h}^{′}(2).$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101532 1074 "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=f(x)+1$ and $v=2g(x).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot 2g(x)-(f(x)+1)\\cdot 2{g}^{′}(x)}{{(2g(x))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-2{g}^{′}(x)(f(x)+1)}{4{g}^{2}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-{g}^{′}(x)(f(x)+1)}{2{g}^{2}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)g(2)-{g}^{′}(2)(f(2)+1)}{2{g}^{2}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1-2(3+1)}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186101942 1064 "Let's suppose we only know the values of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ at $x=0$ along with their derivatives, as given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nCan we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big )$ at $x=0?$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) \\right|_{x=0}\t=\tf'(0)g(0)+f(0)g'(0).\n$$\n\nAll of these values are given in the table! So, substituting the values from the table into the formula, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & ={f}^{'}(0)g(0)+f(0){g}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 false] [17592186101942 1064 "Let's suppose we only know the values of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ at $x=0$ along with their derivatives, as given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nCan we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big )$ at $x=0?$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) \\right|_{x=0}\t=\tf'(0)g(0)+f(0)g'(0).\n$$\n\nAll of these values are given in the table! So, substituting the values from the table into the formula, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & ={f}^{′}(0)g(0)+f(0){g}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445156 1080 "14" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445156 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445156 1083 #uuid "1504691b-9c8e-514b-813d-d8d819372de8" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445157 1080 "-21" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445157 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445157 1083 #uuid "2742eed6-e955-5ad7-b7bb-8036b69a6c7a" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445158 1080 "32" 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445158 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534942 17592186045417 true] [17592186445158 1083 #uuid "37431c2f-97b6-5e35-938b-ebdf9e1e817f